<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Former Shu</h1><p class="st-meta">907 CE – 925 CE · capital Chengdu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom founded in Sichuan by the soldier Wang Jian, a wealthy refuge for Tang scholars and poets in the turmoil after the Tang collapse.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jian-former-shu">Wang Jian</a>, a soldier from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> who had served in the palace army, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in 891 and became military governor of the region. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> made him Prince of Shu in 903. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> deposed the Tang in 907, Wang Jian refused to recognise the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a> and proclaimed himself emperor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Protected by mountains, the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> basin escaped the wars of the north and prospered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>. Many <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> officials and literati took refuge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, among them the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-zhuang">Wei Zhuang</a>, who served as chief minister. The Huajian anthology of song lyrics was compiled there in 940, after the dynasty, but reflects the court culture it fostered.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jian-former-shu">Wang Jian's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-yan-former-shu">Wang Yan</a>, who succeeded in 918, was extravagant and neglected government. In 925 a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a> army marched in and met little resistance; Wang Yan surrendered and was killed with his family in 926 on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> soon became independent again as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-shu">Later Shu</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/former-shu.jpg" alt="Former Shu" width="320" height="437"><figcaption>Image: Pavo Xie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L.LIANG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>907 CE – 925 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chengdu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wang Jian, Wang Yan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism, Daoism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shu (Wang Jian), Wang Shu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=916&amp;focus=former-shu" aria-label="Former Shu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7376 1059 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Former Shu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7696 1315L7898 1125"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7898 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7696 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7696 1315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chengdu. <a href="/atlas?year=916&amp;focus=former-shu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

